Along with the technical proposal, the supplier must submit to the distributor's metering department the following documents:

a) Table corresponding to the meter type and code, found in the ANNEXES of this specification, with the manufacturer's confirmation of the main characteristics and all applicable configurations of the meters for the distributor's approval. Should any feature or configuration available in the meter not be listed in the specific table, the supplier must incorporate it so that we have knowledge of all the functionalities of the offered model. The table approved by the distributor will be requested by the inspector at the beginning of the inspection for the receipt of the meter batch;

b) Complete INMETRO approval ordinance, including all test reports performed, descriptive memorials, meter manual, and other annexes;

c) Complete ANATEL approval ordinance, including all test reports performed, descriptive memorials, device manual, and other annexes when applicable to the code;

d) Information on the conditions for carrying out the type tests referred to in this standard, specifying the tests that can be conducted in the manufacturer's own laboratory, the list of laboratories where the other tests must be performed, as well as the technical description for each test;

e) Document with acceptance of the Warranty terms and compliance with Neoenergia's technical requirements, sent together with the technical and commercial proposal.

f) Provide the manuals of the offered meters and accessories, all in Portuguese, containing at least the following information:
   • Catalog with all technical information of the meter;
   • Outline drawing and dimensions;
   • Detailed drawing of the terminal block, with dimensions;
   • Drawing of the identification plate and the display;
   • Drawing of the internal wiring diagram;
   • Other drawings considered of interest by the supplier;
   • Document containing 360º photos of the equipment, with descriptions of internal and external parts, highlighting parts that have areas of exposed electrical potential (shunts or busbars).



g) Configuration and parameterization spreadsheet for the corresponding models in the ANNEXES, duly filled out by the manufacturer.

It must be equipped with a calendar clock for recognizing the days of the week, leap year, daylight saving time, and separate recording of pulses/quantities under the following conditions:

a) Peak hours;

b) Off-peak hours;

c) Composite hours (sum of peak and off-peak hours);

d) Reserved hours;

e) Intermediate hours (When applicable to the code).

4.4.2.30 O mostrador do medidor deve ser programável do tipo digital conforme 
NBR 14519 e RTM Inmetro específico e deve apresentar:  
a) Código da função e respectiva grandeza com mínimo de 3 dígitos; 
b) Seis dígitos para as respectivas grandezas / pulsos; 
c) Indicação dos postos horários, ponta, fora de ponta, reservado e Intermediário; 
d) Indicação do posto reativo (indutivo e capacitivo), contendo espaçamento entre 
as indicações; 
e) Ponto decimal da grandeza demanda programável para até três casas decimais; 
f) Indicação de emissão de pulso no mostrador ou através de "leds" com as 
respectivas energias.

The meter display must be programmable digital according to NBR 14519 and specific Inmetro RTM and must show:

a) Function code and respective quantity;

b) Six digits for the respective quantities/pulses;

c) Indication of hourly, peak, off-peak, intermediate and reserved periods;

d) Indication of the reactive period (inductive and capacitive), with spacing between the indications;

e) Decimal point of the demand quantity programmable to up to three decimal places;

f) Indication of pulse emission on the display or through LEDs.

For meters with a ratchet register for each flow direction (application in micro and mini-generation), it must obligatorily display reverse registers for the codes that apply simultaneously to both situations (White Tariff and MMGD) in the standardized ABNT 14522 codes, adding a 1 in front of the respective code, for example: 103, 104, 108, etc.

a) Active Energy Totalizer Register;
b) Reactive Energy Totalizer Register;
c) Peak Active Energy Register;
d) Peak Reactive Energy Register;
e) Off-Peak Active Energy Register;
f) Off-Peak Reactive Energy Register;
g) Intermediate Period Active Energy Register;
h) Intermediate Period Reactive Energy Register;
i) Reserved Period Active Energy Register;
j) Reserved Period Reactive Energy Register;
k) Date (in DD/MM/YY format);
l) Time (in HH:MM:SS format);
m) Battery Charging Status;

4.4.4.25 It must be equipped with a calendar clock for recognizing the days of the week, leap year, daylight saving time and separate recording of pulses/quantities under the following conditions:
a) Peak hours;
b) Off-peak hours;
c) Intermediate hours;
d) Composite hours (sum of peak, off-peak, intermediate and reserved hours);
e) Reserved hours.

Meters shortly before or during the term of the contract must undergo the approval stages of the Neoenergia Group. The approval process shall occur within a maximum period of 9 months according to the preliminary schedule below: a) Month zero: Delivery of SAMPLES of the new models to the laboratories, with quantities per meter type to be defined; b) Months one to two: Laboratory evaluation; c) Manufacturing: Period for manufacturing and delivery of meters for field installation; d) Months three to four: Field installation; e) Months five to nine: Performance evaluation; f) Final: Evaluation results. 

Note: Item “c” shall only be initiated after approvals and tests are completed in item “b”. It shall start immediately after the delivery of the batch of new models into the distributors' stocks. The approval time will be frozen due to delivery deadlines of the batch for testing. The supplier must inform the intention to modify the model at the very beginning of the purchasing and approval process; this way, it will be possible to include the intention, name, and quantities in the new supply contract if the supplier is selected to supply the referred codes.

If the new model fails to exhibit acceptable performance, it must meet the following requirements:
 a) Failure rate below 1.0%, excluding parts damaged by lightning strikes or misuse;
 b) The supplier will be required to undertake field evaluation actions to identify possible problems and behaviors exhibited by the new meter models; this is mandatory;
 c) A minimum batch of 300 units will be acquired for field testing;
 d) Data from meters will be collected through inspections, reader reports, or remote reading where possible.

In case of rejection, the supplier must supply the previously approved model until the end of the contract term.

Others - -2 V, I (per phase). Note: The inclusion of reactive time-segment registers must be programmable; the co 02 66/80 ANNEX IV – Meter Profiles with Energy Quality Function – REN871. Objective: Specify the technical requirements for the supply of meters (both direct and indirect) to comply with Resolution 956/2021, with technical characteristics required by ordinance 502/2012 and modules 5 and 8 of PRODIST, which serve as the basis for meeting the sampling process conducted by ANEEL for voltage measurement under steady-state conditions in consumer units of SDBT and SDMT. Specify the requirements for integration into existing Telemetry systems. This supply does not include acquisition of a new collection or management system; the scope is the integration of new hardware into existing platforms. The equipment described in this ANNEX IV must be supplied with the special technical characteristics below and, where not mentioned, shall follow the definitions in previous items of this specification.

Construction Requirements: The construction features must meet the same requirements enforced on other meter codes, ensuring the safety of staff, customers, and accurate recording of consumption and quality data. It must provide availability for a Cellular NIC (CAT-M1/NB and GPRS/3FF) for communication, housed in a dedicated space alongside the meter cabinet, with a protective cover for the NIC (Network Interface Card). External modems are not within the scope of this specification. Modem supply will be handled in a separate procurement process under separate codes. This item will be evaluated along procurement and scheduling phases. The modem power supply must be provided by the meter itself; connection of external cables to other power sources, or externally connecting to the optical or serial port of the meter is not permitted. The meter's internal power supply must be sized appropriately to support its own consumption plus the modem without increasing internal losses.

The NIC must allow the use of a standard SMA external antenna for connection when an amplifier or gain antenna is needed to boost the cellular signal.

Technical Requirements – Technical Characteristics:
The equipment must comply with the following:
(a) Supply voltage – Auto Range from ±85V to ±254V (Phase + Neutral);
(b) Cellular modem CAT-M1/NB and GPRS – Quad-band, multi-operator capable;
(c) Comply with requirements imposed by Normative Resolution 956 of 2021;
(d) Comply with requirements imposed by Normative Resolution 502 of 2012;
(e) Hold INMETRO certification related to requirements set forth in ordinance 502/2012, PRODIST modules 5 and 8.

Characteristics for Consumption and Supply Quality Measurement:
(a) Must record active electrical energy consumption in at least four tariff registers, with programmable start and end of each register;
(b) Information related to consumer usage must be available via a display on the meter itself or remotely;
(c) The accumulated active energy value per tariff register;
(d) Indicate the current tariff register identification;
(e) Comply with Article 3 of ordinance 502/2012;
(f) Comply with PRODIST 5, section 5.2 “MEASUREMENT SYSTEMS USED FOR DATA COLLECTION AND DETERMINATION OF ELECTRIC ENERGY QUALITY PARAMETERS – QEE”;
(g) Comply with PRODIST 8, section 8.1 - PRODUCT QUALITY, which defines terminology, characterizes phenomena, establishes indicators and limits or reference values relating to voltage conformity under steady-state and waveform voltage disturbances;
(h) Hardware and software must include procedures and technologies that assure data security, especially personal data collected from consumer units;
(i) Allow configuration of voltage values as per “ANNEX I: Voltage Classification Ranges – Steady-State Voltages (PRODIST 8)” depending on installation voltage;
(j) Allow voltage reference changes via local and remote software;
(k) Allow sample quantity adjustments via local and remote software;
(l) Allow sample timing adjustments via local and remote software;
(m) Indicate via event generation individual phase or total absence of voltage, reporting to the NIC through integration interface;
(n) Store absence and missing voltage alarms in memory;
(o) Have a battery to maintain serial communication for up to two minutes;

Measurement systems used for billing users connected at medium and high voltage distribution must at least:
(p) Be capable of determining, for consumption and/or generation according to user features, the following quantities:
  i. Active energy in kWh;
  ii. Integrated demand with programmable intervals from 5 to 60 minutes, in kW;
  iii. Reactive demand in kVAr and reactive energy in kVAr;
(q) Have:
  i. Mass memory capable of storing data of active energy, reactive energy, voltage and optionally active and reactive demand, separated for consumed and injected amounts when needed. Integralization interval can be fixed at 5 minutes or programmable between 5 and 60 minutes, storing data for at least 37 days;
  ii. Interface for local acquisition of measured values, mass memory data, and quality indicators in open format;
  iii. If applicable, time synchronization mechanism via command from the data acquisition central or optionally by GPS;
  iv. Meter with alphanumeric identification of at least 14 digits;
  v. Pulse output for demand controller.

Display Information Presentation: The meter must be capable of showing on its display or remote display the following information per ordinance 502/2012:
(r) Voltage and current values of each phase;
(s) Accumulated active electrical energy per tariff register;
(t) Identification of the current tariff register, if applicable;
(u) Date and time of start and end of short and long interruptions from the last 3 months;
(v) The last 12 calculated values of the indicators Relative Duration of Critical Voltage Transgression (DRP) and Relative Duration of Severe Voltage Transgression (DRC).

Information items from points “a” to “e” must be available through specific acquisition outputs on the meter.

Communication Requirements – Cellular Modem (NIC):
The NIC supplied must meet requirements below, besides technical items in DIS-ETE-036:
(a) Neoenergia will indicate the meter models to which the NIC logical and construction part must be integrated;
(b) Must be a cellular modem CAT-M1/NB and GPRS/3FF integrated with the meter via NIC;
(c) NIC must support CAT-M1, CAT-NB2, GPRS/EDGE bands and bands B28, B3, B87, B31;
(d) Support private APNs of Neoenergia for all operators;
(e) Be coupled to the meter via NIC (Network Interface Communication);
(f) Allow SIM card 3FF or modem replacement without disconnecting meter power supply;
(g) In case of phase loss or absence in meter power supply, the modem must report absence via cellular network to MDC before modem shutdown;
(h) Modem must have supercapacitor, battery or similar to guarantee autonomy sufficient to transmit data on power failure or absence;
(i) Other technical requirements per ETE-036 – Communication Modem;
(j) Allow local and remote firmware updates of meter and modem as needed;
(k) Modem hardware must be integrated with current MDC platform.

Integration Requirements – Cellular Modem, MDC, and MDM:
Meter supplier must provide documentation and support for protocol integration of meter into MDC and MDM on current platforms at each company side, informing all rules, parameterization commands, requests, OBIS commands, protocols for measurement, quality, events, alarms, firmware updates, and other necessary items for collection and operation with MDC and MDM. Some hours should be considered for meetings, development or firmware customizations. Documentation must be delivered along with the technical proposal for procurement. For modem integration authentication on access servers and standard command protocols, the meter/modem supplier is responsible for obtaining relevant protocol information from MDM and MDC system suppliers to handle contractual negotiations.

Requirement of Environment for Data Collection:
The supplier of meters with NIC CAT-M1/NB and GPRS must consider in the technical proposal the integration of equipment into Neoenergia systems as per this specification. NICs and meters supplied must be integrated into telemetry systems, meeting the following:
Administrative:
- Allow online communication management for sending commands for testing, configurations, and equipment registration;
- Provide data collected to Neoenergia.
Functional:
- Export individual or grouped meter reports and spreadsheets;
- Send commands to collect meter and modem parameters;
- Provide DRP and DRC integralized by the meter;
- Allow voltage reference alteration (QEE);
- Collect consumption registries;
- Enable date and time change of the meter;
- Perform firmware upgrades of meter and modem (upon consultation);
- Send additional commands to field devices;
- Relay activation;
- Date and time synchronization;
- Alarm reading and indication from meters;
- Mass memory reading;
- Reset DRP/DRC, DIC/FIC, and VTCD;
- Configuration of voltage reference, sample quantity, and sampling time;
- Parameterization of quantities displayed on the display in normal mode;
- Parameterization of tariff registers and holiday calendars.

For collection points with problems (NIC failure, meter, or signal level), the supplier must provide on-site support for problem assessment and correction. Supplier must provide a minimum amount of support hours for instability, operational doubts, and problem corrections obstructing data collection.

The meter and NIC supplier must guarantee integration with Neoenergia MDC systems, providing documentation and support to complete integration so that meter/NIC and MDC system protocols integrate successfully.

Support:
Support must cover:
- Opening and follow-up of calls;
- Remote problem verification and resolution;
- Technical assistance regarding system operation and use.
Service Hours:
- Support available Monday through Friday (except local Brazilian holidays), from 9:00 to 18:00 Brasília time (GMT-3, including daylight saving adjustments).
- Support accessible via email, phone, or web portal.

Linked Documents:
(a) MDM and MDC System Requirements Specification (ER REN 871) versions 5.4 dated 21/08/2020;
(b) Technical Specification for Telemetry Remotes DIS-ETE-036;
(c) Technical Meter Specification ESP DISTRIBU-ENGE-033_rev3.1; IBERDROLA NETWORKS SPECIFICATION - INS 97.20.06.

SAP Codes and Equipment Descriptions:
- ELEKTRO MED BI 3F DIR 15/100A B GB 37086 802254: Bifasic Direct meter, REN 871 SMART 871 electronic energy meter, bifasic, 3 wires, 2 elements, measurement of active and reactive energy, voltage 120/240V, current 15A min, 120A max, 60Hz, accuracy class B, additional requirements: quality measurement (PRODIST 5 and 8), remote cut and reconnection, 4 quadrant measurement, optical communication port, NIC communication, neutral current, load absence and FIC, 37-day mass memory, AMR and AMI applications, internal battery and supercap for RTC, LCD display; compliant with ABNT NBR 14.519, 14.520, RTM586/2012; supports remote monitoring (telemetry) NIC; communication via bidirectional SMS packets and GPRS (TCP/IP) over GSM/3G/4G; intelligent protocol compatibility (ABNT/DLMS), RS232 serial channel for communication; software/SMS configurable; NIC integrated in dedicated slot with cellular operator chip. Further technical information per telemeter specifications and NIC acquisition.

- ELEKTRO MED MON 2F DIR 15/100A B GB 37109 0802253: Single-phase, 2 wires, 1 element, active/reactive energy measurement, other features same as above.

- ELEKTRO MED TRI 4F IND 2,5/10A C GA 37111 0802252: Indirect three-phase, 4 wires, 3 elements, programmable multifunction, voltage 120/240V, current 5A min, 10A max, 50-60Hz, accuracy class C or D, quality measurement, 16 channels mass memory, demand registration, I/O interfaces, RS232, DLMS communication; other similar features.

- ELEKTRO MED TRI 4F DIR 15/100A B GB 37110 0802255: Direct three-phase, 4 wires, 3 elements, features similar to above.

- ELEKTRO MED MON 3F DIR 15/100A B 37422: Single-phase 3 wires, 1 element, active energy direct and reverse measurement, voltage 240V, 15A min, 100A max, accuracy B, quality measurement, remote cut and reclosure, 4 quadrant measurement, optical communication, NIC, neutral current, load absence, 37-day mass memory, AMR and AMI, internal battery/supercap, LCD display, ABNT compliance, communication via SMS/GPRS over Cat-M1/NB networks.

- Indirect Group Meter – MED INT MULT PROG 3F 37112: Three-phase indirect, 4 wires, 3 elements, programmable multifunction, voltage 48/280V, current 0.5A min, 10A max, accuracy class C, additional requirements including quality measurement, rigid base, transparent cover, line of load feeding, 60Hz inductive and capacitive, 0.5% accuracy, NIC communication, DLMS, over-installation, Group B application, remote communication RF, compliant with RTM587/2012, NIC telemetry with SMS bidirectional and GPRS.

The NIC must be integrated via software/SMS configuration in dedicated NIC slot and contain cellular operator chip. Further technical details per telemetry and NIC acquisition technical specifications DIS-ETE-036.
